What This Portfolio Demonstrates
A student portfolio can include much more than polished final maps. The materials preserved here include:

class projects and technical exercises;
independent and collaborative research;
field data collection and database design;
maps developed through multiple rounds of revision;
reports, presentations, posters, and conference materials;
volunteer GIS and professional service;
funded graduate assistantship projects;
reflections on what worked, what changed, and what I would approach differently today.

The artifacts reflect the software, technology, data, and cartographic practices available when they were created. Original work is presented honestly, with newly written context added to explain its purpose and significance.
About This Portfolio

A Note for Students
This site is an example, not a required formula. A strong portfolio does not need to contain the same number or types of projects. Its purpose is to help visitors understand:

what problem or question you addressed;
what role you played;
what data, methods, and tools you used;
what you produced or discovered;
what you learned from the experience.

The strongest portfolio is one that clearly and accurately represents your own development.

Project Context
The assignment provided an opportunity to practice cartographic design while working with an unusual geographic setting and data source. Rather than mapping a location on Earth, I used planetary imagery and rover-position information to document SPIRIT’s movement after landing in Gusev Crater.
The completed poster included:

a small-scale locator map showing Gusev Crater on Mars;
a regional map relating Gusev Crater to Elysium Mons and Ma’adim Vallis;
an enlarged view of Gusev Crater;
a detailed map of the rover traverse;
the locations of the lander, backshell, parachute, and heat-shield impact;
descriptive text explaining the mission and mapping process.

Solving the Coordinate Problem
The most significant technical challenge was correctly placing the rover path on the Martian surface.
The rover-position data from the Mars Exploration Rover Analyst’s Notebook used x- and y-values measured in meters from a local origin at the landing site. Those values did not use the origin of the map’s coordinate system, so importing them directly would have placed the traverse in the wrong location.
I addressed this by:

determining the mapped coordinates of the SPIRIT landing site in ArcMap;
importing the rover’s original x- and y-values into Microsoft Excel;
adding the landing-site coordinate offsets to the rover-position values;
importing the adjusted coordinates into ArcMap;
checking the resulting traverse against the landing site and surrounding imagery.

Applying those offsets moved the rover track into its correct mapped position and allowed me to complete the poster.
